// Solver and shape settings for the physics world (ADR-002). One place, so that nothing about contact is a
// number buried in the wrapper. Contact friction and restitution are not here: they are gameplay tuning and
// come from `Tuning.contact` (walls), `Tuning.marker` (tyres) and `Tuning.carContact` (two cars).

/** Fixed solver iteration counts for every step. Never varied at run time (ADR-002 rule 2). */
export const VELOCITY_ITERATIONS = 8
export const POSITION_ITERATIONS = 3

// Units: planck runs at true sim scale — metres, a 0.40 m car — with no length scaling in the wrapper. Its
// fixed tolerances are 5 mm of contact slop and a 10 mm polygon skin (1.25 % and 2.5 % of the car's length).
// Contact was firm at this size in experiment E-01 and is firm here: tests/sim/physics/ holds a car driven
// flat out into a wall, and one fired at it at 60 m/s. If that ever stops being true, scale lengths inside
// world.ts only and record the factor here.

/**
 * The car's collision box has its four corners cut off by this much along each side, so that a corner slides
 * off a wall or another car instead of hooking on it (ADR-002 rule 3).
 */
export const CORNER_CHAMFER_M = 0.02

/**
 * Wall vertices closer together than this are merged before a chain is built. planck needs neighbouring chain
 * vertices to be further apart than its contact slop (5 mm), or an edge has no usable direction.
 */
export const MIN_WALL_VERTEX_GAP_M = 0.006

/**
 * A loose tyre (F-308) that is within this of where it lay, and slower than this, is put exactly there and left
 * asleep: two millimetres is a fifth of a screen pixel. Without it a tyre would creep home for ever and never
 * stop costing a step.
 */
export const TYRE_REST_M = 0.002
export const TYRE_REST_MS = 0.02

/**
 * A tyre given no more travel than planck's contact slop (5 mm) cannot be held at a limit planck can tell from
 * where it started: it is a post, and is made as one.
 */
export const MIN_TYRE_TRAVEL_M = 0.005

/**
 * What a tyre weighs while a car holds it against the end of its travel: enough that nothing a car can do moves
 * it, so that it stands exactly as a fixed marker does. It stays a moving body in planck's eyes, which keeps its
 * contact with the car, and what the solver has learnt about that contact, from one step to the next.
 */
export const HELD_TYRE_MASS_KG = 1e6
/** And how hard it is to turn while it is held, kg·m²: it does not roll off the car's corner any more, it is a post. */
export const HELD_TYRE_SPIN_KG_M2 = 1e6

/** A tyre within this of the end of its travel counts as being at it: a millimetre. */
export const TYRE_LIMIT_SKIN_M = 0.001

/**
 * A car holds a tyre at the end of its travel when it presses it outward this many times harder than the tyre's
 * own spring pulls it back there. More than once, so that a tyre whose spring is leaning it on a parked car is
 * not taken for one that a car is pushing: the two press on each other exactly as hard as the spring pulls.
 */
export const TYRE_HOLD_MARGIN = 1.5

/**
 * A tyre put at the end of its travel waits there, held, for up to this many steps for the car that was pushing it.
 * It is put there up to a step before the car gets there, and planck does not report a touch until the car's nose
 * is within its 10 mm skin again: let go in between, the tyre would be light when the car arrived.
 */
export const TYRE_ARRIVAL_TICKS = 3
